{
  "name": "cloud_visibility",
  "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
  "description": "What actually makes §25's drawn cloud legible from a top-down camera: the cloud's own body (§25d's recalibration) or the SHADOW it casts (§23c, which ships off). Asked because raising the body alone runs into a ceiling that is a property of the art rather than of the calibration — a cloud is near-white, RimWorld's lit desert sand is about 0.78 white, and no alpha can put more than about 46/255 of red between them. A shadow has no such ceiling: it darkens ground that is already bright, which is the direction with room in it, and it is how a cloud is legible in any real top-down view. Four frames per hour so 'the cloud has a body now' and 'the ground under it went dark' stay separate claims, and so the combination can be judged rather than assumed to be the sum.",
